ight now and you  don't feel it but it's holding the  galaxies together  we think that is possible that if you  take two dark matter particles they can  annihilate and create something from the  standard model which is all of the  things we understand all the particles  we know about and so on and so if they  can annihilate like that then what  happens is they come together and  they'll produce some kind of shower of  particles high-energy particles and  that'll happen more often in places  where dark matter is really concentrated  so the power the Dark Matter  annihilation power is proportional to  the cross-section inversely proportional  to mass and proportional to the density  Square and that means higher the density  the higher the power by a lot and so you  expect that in places where dark matter  gathers in these Dark Matter halos there  should be a lot of annihilation  happening in the center and a lot of  these high-energy particles being  produced  these halos these Dark Matter clumps are  forming the first galaxies and the first  stars they're bringing in the gas that  forms the first stars and galaxies in  the universe and if something is  happening in the centres of these halos  if you have this big sort of heat source  then that can push the gas out of the  halos and delay when the first stars and  galaxies form
 

